## Experiment 3: Responsive Food Ordering Interface

In this experiment, the Food Ordering App interface was improved to support different screen sizes and orientations. Responsive UI allows an application to automatically adjust its layout according to the available screen size. This is important for a food ordering application because users may access the application using different mobile devices and screen dimensions.

MediaQuery was used to obtain information such as screen width, screen height, orientation, and other device-related properties. The available screen width was used to adjust the size of food cards, images, containers, and other interface components.

LayoutBuilder was also used to create layouts based on the constraints provided by the parent widget. For example, the food menu can display items differently depending on the available width. Expanded and Flexible widgets were used where appropriate to make better use of available space and reduce layout overflow problems.

The responsive design was tested using different screen sizes and orientations. The main objective was to make sure that important elements such as food images, names, prices, buttons, restaurant information, and cart controls remain accessible and properly arranged.

